## Method and reasoning
Choose the first compatible screening in order of finish time. In any optimum you can replace its first screening with the earliest finisher, because the replacement leaves at least as much time for every later screening. Apply the argument to what remains.

## Walk through the sample
Accept [1,3), [3,5), [5,6) in that order. Equality between an end and the next start is allowed. Taking [2,4) instead blocks both of the first two screenings.

## Complexity
Time O(n log n), space O(n).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<pair<int, int>> a(n);
    for (auto &[r, l] : a)
        cin >> l >> r;
    sort(a.begin(), a.end());
    int end = INT_MIN, answer = 0;
    for (auto [r, l] : a)
        if (l >= end) {
            end = r;
            ++answer;
        }
    cout << answer << '\n';
}
~~~
